WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.371 --> 00:00:03.381
(serious music)

00:00:03.381 --> 00:00:04.510
- [Narrator] Police officers in Canada are

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:06.884
trained as problem solvers.

00:00:06.884 --> 00:00:09.467
(upbeat music)

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:13.470
So the more tools they can
carry in their toolkit,

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:15.190
the more opportunities to problem solve,

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:17.680
and sometimes it's about
building the relationship.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.088
- What's your name?

00:00:19.088 --> 00:00:20.577
Nick, you know what?

00:00:20.577 --> 00:00:22.548
That was absolutely
perfect, what you just did.

00:00:22.548 --> 00:00:23.878
You stopped, you looked both ways.

00:00:23.878 --> 00:00:27.017
Because of that, I'm giving
you a Positive Ticket.

00:00:27.017 --> 00:00:29.798
- My name is Ward Clapham,
I'm a 28-year veteran

00:00:29.798 --> 00:00:31.678
of the Royal Canadian Mounted Police,

00:00:31.678 --> 00:00:34.651
and I'm the creator of the
Positive Ticket Program.

00:00:34.651 --> 00:00:36.800
(engine whirring)

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.010
- Nanaimo is a community of
approximately 100,000 people.

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:41.730
It's a great place,
and it's something that

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:43.018
everybody should come and see.

00:00:43.018 --> 00:00:45.338
Since the inception of the
program of Positive Ticketing,

00:00:45.338 --> 00:00:47.058
we've issued approximately
sixty-four hundred

00:00:47.058 --> 00:00:48.520
tickets to youth.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.418
- [Police Officer] I'm giving
out Positive Tickets, okay?

00:00:50.418 --> 00:00:51.528
Do you know what a Positive Ticket is?

00:00:51.528 --> 00:00:54.017
- [Ward] The Positive Ticket
program, it's about catching

00:00:54.017 --> 00:00:55.138
kids doing things right.

00:00:55.138 --> 00:00:56.928
Rewarding them for good behavior.

00:00:56.928 --> 00:00:58.448
It could be for using a crosswalk.

00:00:58.448 --> 00:01:01.865
- [Police Officer] Good job, there ya go!

00:01:04.594 --> 00:01:05.894
- [Ward] Wearing a helmet
when you're skateboarding

00:01:05.894 --> 00:01:06.961
or riding your bike.

00:01:06.961 --> 00:01:08.514
It could be for doing your homework.

00:01:08.514 --> 00:01:10.764
And for many officers,
it's the ice breaker

00:01:10.764 --> 00:01:12.604
to approach young people.

00:01:12.604 --> 00:01:13.945
- Here, I'm gonna give
you a Positive Ticket.

00:01:13.945 --> 00:01:15.834
Here you go, Nico, thank you very much.

00:01:15.834 --> 00:01:17.836
Mohammed, very nice to meet you, Mohammed.

00:01:17.836 --> 00:01:20.494
- The Positive Ticket
idea really exemplifies

00:01:20.494 --> 00:01:21.743
the Canadian values.

00:01:21.743 --> 00:01:23.854
We're recognized as being really polite.

00:01:23.854 --> 00:01:24.817
Super polite.

00:01:24.817 --> 00:01:26.453
- Well that's for you.

00:01:26.453 --> 00:01:27.747
- [Mother] What do you say?

00:01:27.747 --> 00:01:28.580
- Thanks.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:29.616
- Well, you're very welcome.

00:01:29.616 --> 00:01:31.216
- [Ward] It's not about the ticket.

00:01:31.216 --> 00:01:33.067
It's all about the relationship.

00:01:33.067 --> 00:01:34.597
Because you see, what we were finding

00:01:34.597 --> 00:01:38.427
was that the ticket for many
young people, it was a trophy.

00:01:38.427 --> 00:01:41.104
It was a recognition that
they were a good person,

00:01:41.104 --> 00:01:42.555
and they had never had a police officer,

00:01:42.555 --> 00:01:43.878
let alone, maybe a caring adult,

00:01:43.878 --> 00:01:45.867
tell them that they were worthy,

00:01:45.867 --> 00:01:49.107
and that there was something
special about them.

00:01:49.107 --> 00:01:50.246
- We had a whole pile of kids,

00:01:50.246 --> 00:01:53.256
and they're all pretty excited to see me.

00:01:53.256 --> 00:01:54.846
There was actually one kid in particular,

00:01:54.846 --> 00:01:56.547
she was pretty shy, pretty reserved,

00:01:56.547 --> 00:01:58.606
and dad was telling me, he says,

00:01:58.606 --> 00:02:00.198
yeah, my daughter, who is in kindergarten,

00:02:00.198 --> 00:02:04.317
so five years old, is scared
to death of the police.

00:02:04.317 --> 00:02:05.224
And he says, I've been trying to tell her,

00:02:05.224 --> 00:02:07.584
we're in Canada now, it's safe.

00:02:07.584 --> 00:02:09.003
The police are good.

00:02:09.003 --> 00:02:10.351
I watched her cross the street.

00:02:10.351 --> 00:02:13.931
She did exactly what I’ve
been trying to teach the kids.

00:02:13.931 --> 00:02:15.472
And so I gave her the Positive Ticket.

00:02:15.472 --> 00:02:17.601
So then she got a big smile.

00:02:17.601 --> 00:02:19.723
That's why I do this.

00:02:19.723 --> 00:02:21.563
- [Ward] Since the
program started in 2002,

00:02:21.563 --> 00:02:23.690
over a million Positive
Tickets have been given out

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.051
by police around the world.

00:02:26.051 --> 00:02:30.512
Even if one percent of
those million tickets

00:02:30.512 --> 00:02:32.923
turned into life-changing
events for young people,

00:02:32.923 --> 00:02:34.512
that's a heck of a difference.

00:02:34.512 --> 00:02:37.095
(upbeat music)

